(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-venous-disorders-vignettes"]={"title":"Venous Disorders Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with symptomatic varicose veins has worn stockings and exercised faithfully for several months with persistent aching. Duplex ultrasound confirms great saphenous vein reflux. Which is the most appropriate next treatment?","choices":["Surgical valvuloplasty","Venous ablation therapy","Lifelong observation only","Oral flavonoid therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stockings are tried for weeks to months before venous ablation is offered, and the goal is to improve symptoms and appearance. She has now failed that trial, with reflux confirmed. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man starts outpatient treatment for deep vein thrombosis with low molecular weight heparin injections overlapped with warfarin. The injections continue for at least five days and until his international normalized ratio is above which value?","choices":["Above 3","Above 4","Above 2","Above 1"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Parenteral therapy overlaps with warfarin for at least five days and until the international normalized ratio is above 2, when warfarin has reached its therapeutic effect and the heparin can stop. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has a catheter-related deep vein thrombosis of the right arm and asks how likely a pulmonary embolism is compared with a leg clot. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["Up to 6%, versus 15 to 30% for leg clots","15 to 30%, versus up to 6% for leg clots","About 3% for arm and leg clots alike","Under 1% for arm clots, 6% for leg clots"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary embolism occurs in up to 6% of upper-extremity deep vein thromboses, versus 15% to 30% of lower-extremity clots. Arm clots are only 5% to 10% of all deep vein thromboses. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with active pancreatic cancer, which supplies the hypercoagulable component of the Virchow triad, has an acute proximal deep vein thrombosis of the left leg. She also has active gastrointestinal bleeding. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Systemic thrombolysis","Outpatient rivaroxaban","Inferior vena cava filter","Unfractionated heparin infusion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An inferior vena cava filter is used for acute proximal lower-extremity deep vein thrombosis when there is active bleeding or anticoagulation is otherwise contraindicated. The filter keeps the clot from traveling to the lungs and causing pulmonary embolism. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man developed a single large arteriovenous fistula of the forearm after a penetrating injury. It causes swelling and a bruit over the arm. Which is the management for this acquired fistula?","choices":["Surgical removal or size reduction","Compression stockings alone","Warm compresses and elevation","Lifelong anticoagulation alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acquired fistulas, whether from trauma or from a procedure, are managed surgically by removing the fistula or decreasing its size. Embolization is an option sometimes used for congenital fistulas. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with symptomatic varicose veins has a duplex ultrasound showing significant saphenous vein reflux. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Vascular specialist","Cardiologist","Hematologist","Orthopedic surgeon"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Significant saphenous reflux is one of the triggers for referral to a vascular specialist, who can offer the options for treating reflux. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man receiving an unfractionated heparin infusion for deep vein thrombosis develops life-threatening bleeding. Which agent reverses the heparin?","choices":["Protamine sulfate","Idarucizumab","Andexanet alfa","Vitamin K by mouth"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Protamine sulfate is the reversal agent for unfractionated heparin and low molecular weight heparin when bleeding is life-threatening. Each anticoagulant has its own specific reversal agent. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with diabetes has a painful ulcer over the lateral ankle. His foot is cool and the pedal pulses are weak. Which test should be obtained next?","choices":["Punch biopsy of the ulcer","Ankle-brachial index","Venous duplex ultrasound","Catheter-based venography"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A painful lateral ankle ulcer with weak pulses suggests arterial insufficiency, and the arterial pulse examination with the ankle-brachial index is performed on any lower extremity wound to look for it. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man develops a first calf deep vein thrombosis one week after minor knee arthroscopy under a local nerve block, followed by a long car trip. Which duration of anticoagulation is appropriate?","choices":["A fixed three months","Indefinitely","Six weeks","Three to six months"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A clot provoked by a minor transient risk factor, such as estrogen therapy, pregnancy, minor surgery, prolonged travel or minor leg injury, is treated for three to six months, chosen from the patient's history. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman has symptomatic varicose veins that did not improve with stockings. She prefers a nonsurgical procedure in which a solution is injected to scar and close the veins. Which procedure fits?","choices":["Vein stripping","Radiofrequency ablation","Venous bypass","Sclerotherapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sclerotherapy is the injection of a sclerosing agent to fibrose and obliterate the veins, one of the interventions for varicose veins along with stripping and catheter-based ablation. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 24-year-old soccer player comes to urgent care with left calf pain that began two days ago when he felt a sudden pull while sprinting, and he has bruising around the ankle. He has no leg swelling, tenderness along the deep veins, cancer, cast or history of clots. By the Wells criteria, a calf muscle tear is at least as likely as deep vein thrombosis. Which is the most appropriate first test?","choices":["D-dimer blood test","Venous duplex ultrasound","Contrast ascending venography","Computed tomography of the leg"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The likelier alternative diagnosis subtracts 2 from his Wells score, giving a low likelihood. Low-likelihood patients start with a D-dimer, and a negative result lets the clinician stop, since D-dimer is a sensitive rule-out test. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has a first deep vein thrombosis of the left leg. Genetic testing shows factor V Leiden, an inherited clotting abnormality. Which component of the Virchow triad does this finding represent?","choices":["Venous insufficiency","Hypercoagulable state","Venous stasis","Endothelial trauma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Factor V Leiden is an inherited hypercoagulable state, like prothrombin gene mutation and protein C, protein S or antithrombin deficiency. It makes the blood clot too readily, the hypercoagulable component of the triad. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has long-standing pitting edema of the lower leg with brown pigmentation and a healed medial ankle ulcer. Duplex ultrasound shows thickened, scarred valve leaflets and chronic obstruction of the femoral vein. He does not recall any leg clot. Which is the most likely cause of his condition?","choices":["Congenital arteriovenous fistula","Pelvic vein neoplastic obstruction","Prior deep vein thrombosis","Progressive superficial reflux"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Scarred valve leaflets with chronic proximal obstruction are post-thrombotic changes, and prior deep vein thrombosis is the leading cause even though about a quarter of patients recall no clot. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has visible, dilated, tortuous veins of the thigh and calf that are tender to palpation, with aching heaviness after prolonged standing. Which is the most common predisposing factor for her condition?","choices":["Recent pregnancy","Regular heavy lifting","Family history of varicose veins","Prolonged standing at work"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The most common predisposing factor for varicose veins is family history, so ask whether relatives have them, and how old they were when they developed. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman had a left leg deep vein thrombosis 11 months ago. She now has chronic left leg heaviness, edema and brownish ankle skin that worsen with standing and improve with elevation. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Recurrent deep vein thrombosis","Lymphedema","Drug-induced edema","Post-thrombotic syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Post-thrombotic (postphlebitic) syndrome is symptomatic chronic venous insufficiency that follows a deep vein thrombosis, appearing 6 to 24 months later, with heaviness, edema and brownish skin change from venous hypertension. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with metastatic breast cancer on active treatment presents to the emergency department with a swollen right leg. The entire leg is swollen and the right calf measures 4 cm more than the left. No alternative diagnosis is judged as likely as deep vein thrombosis, and no other Wells criteria are present. Which is the best next test?","choices":["Ankle-brachial index","Venous duplex ultrasound","D-dimer assay first","Contrast ascending venography"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Active cancer, a swollen entire leg and calf swelling over 3 cm give a Wells score of 3, meaning thrombosis is likely. High-probability patients skip the D-dimer and go straight to ultrasound. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman on chemotherapy for breast cancer comes to the emergency department with a calf 4 cm larger than the other, tenderness along the deep veins and no better explanation, so her Wells score is high. Her D-dimer, drawn at triage, is negative. Which action should be avoided?","choices":["Ordering a venous duplex ultrasound","Asking about shortness of breath","Measuring both calf circumferences","Ending the work-up at the D-dimer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With a high Wells score the D-dimer is skipped, or if it was drawn, a negative result does not end the evaluation: she still goes to venous duplex ultrasound. Stopping here risks missing a clot with high pretest probability. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has a large arteriovenous fistula of the thigh and a positive Nicoladoni-Branham sign (slowing of the heart rate when the fistula is compressed). Her resting pulse is 88 beats per minute. Which action should the examiner avoid?","choices":["Feeling for a thrill over it","Checking pulses below it","Holding the fistula compressed","Listening over it for a bruit"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Compressing a large fistula reflexly slows the heart rate, so sustained manual compression risks a significant fall in pulse and should be avoided during the examination. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man hospitalized for six days with pneumonia develops swelling of the left calf. His team considers using the Wells criteria to estimate his probability of deep vein thrombosis. Which statement about applying the Wells criteria to him is accurate?","choices":["Meant for outpatient or emergency use","Most accurate in hospitalized patients","Designed for upper-extremity clots","A low score replaces all testing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The Wells criteria are used in the outpatient and emergency department settings, not for inpatients, in whom the score is less useful. A hospitalized patient needs clinical judgment and ultrasound rather than this pathway. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man has been on strict bed rest for six days during an admission for pneumonia and now has swelling and aching of the entire left leg. Which component of the Virchow triad does his prolonged bed rest represent?","choices":["Endothelial trauma","Arterial vasospasm","Venous stasis","Hypercoagulable state"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bed rest is immobilization, which alters blood flow and lets blood pool in the leg veins, the venous stasis component of the Virchow triad. Prolonged immobilization raises the risk of deep vein thrombosis about twenty-fold. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman with a previous deep vein thrombosis two years ago comes to the emergency department with swelling of the entire right leg. She has no pitting edema, calf asymmetry over 3 cm, tenderness along the deep veins, collateral veins, cancer, recent bed rest, surgery or cast, and no better alternative diagnosis. By the Wells criteria, which likelihood category applies?","choices":["Low (score below 1)","High (score of 3 or more)","Moderate (score of 3 to 4)","Moderate (score of 1 to 2)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A previously documented deep vein thrombosis adds 1 point and swelling of the entire leg adds 1 point, for 2 in total, the moderate band of 1 to 2. Moderate likelihood leads to a D-dimer next. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has a positive D-dimer during evaluation of calf pain two weeks after a bicycle crash with extensive bruising. He asks why his clinician says the result cannot confirm a clot. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["It rises only with pulmonary embolism","It rises only with thigh clots","It reflects fibrinolysis from many causes","It is specific but not sensitive"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. D-dimer elevation implies increased fibrinolysis, but it is sensitive and not specific. Bruising, surgery, cancer and many other conditions raise it, so a positive result needs imaging while a negative one helps rule out a clot. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with a peripherally inserted central catheter for chemotherapy develops a tender cord in the upper arm. Venous duplex ultrasound shows extensive clot burden in a superficial vein. He has active cancer and a prior deep vein thrombosis. Which therapy should be added to compresses and an anti-inflammatory drug?","choices":["Aspirin alone","Anticoagulation","Systemic corticosteroids","Broad-spectrum antibiotics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. With extensive clot burden or an intermediate or higher risk of thrombosis, anticoagulation may be started, using unfractionated heparin, low molecular weight heparin such as enoxaparin, or fondaparinux. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has a painful ulcer above the medial malleolus with a clean base, fibrinous exudate and serous drainage, surrounded by brown pigmentation and pitting edema. Her pedal pulses are strong and her ankle-brachial index is 1.10. Which measure is essential for the ulcer to heal?","choices":["Topical antibiotic dressings alone","Oral flavonoid therapy alone","Early surgical valvuloplasty","Compression with edema control"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Venous ulcers will not heal unless edema is controlled and compression is applied, and an ankle-brachial index of 1.10 shows compression is safe. Wound care referral supports this. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has a congenital arteriovenous fistula of the calf with numerous small communications, causing chronic swelling and aching. She has no signs of ischemia or heart failure. Which is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["Ligation of every communication","Elastic support hose","Surgical excision of the fistula","Lifelong anticoagulation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Congenital fistulas are difficult to treat because the communications can be numerous, so they are managed conservatively with elastic support hose once peripheral artery disease is excluded. Embolization sometimes removes one. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with a diagnosed leg deep vein thrombosis, not yet treated, develops sudden shortness of breath and pleuritic chest pain. Which complication has occurred?","choices":["Acute myocardial infarction","Pulmonary embolism","Post-thrombotic syndrome","Septic thrombophlebitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Part of the thrombus can break off and travel to the lungs, causing a pulmonary embolism, the most important complication of deep vein thrombosis. Sudden breathlessness and pleuritic pain in this setting should prompt that concern. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with uncomplicated superficial thrombophlebitis of the forearm after an intravenous catheter is discharged on warm compresses, elevation and an anti-inflammatory drug. He is anxious that the clot could travel to his lungs. Which follow-up instruction is most appropriate?","choices":["Reexamination in 24 to 48 hours","Reexamination in 7 to 10 days","Reexamination in 6 to 8 weeks","Return only if fever develops"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A repeat examination within 7 to 10 days checks for resolution versus progression, and persistence or worsening prompts a repeat duplex ultrasound. He should return sooner if it worsens. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with chronic venous insufficiency and recurrent ulcers is being considered for an invasive venous procedure. Which test is the gold standard for defining his venous anatomy beforehand?","choices":["Catheter-based venography","Venous duplex ultrasound","Cross-sectional venography","Ankle-brachial index"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Catheter-based venography is the gold standard, rarely needed to make the diagnosis, and is performed before an invasive intervention to define the anatomy. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has dilated superficial leg veins with dull aching after standing. He has no edema, skin changes or ulceration, and his pedal pulses are normal. Which is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["Laser vein ablation","Vein sclerotherapy","Vein stripping surgery","Stockings and exercise"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Isolated varicose veins without edema, skin changes or ulceration are managed conservatively at first, using leg elevation, exercise and compression once arterial disease has been ruled out. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman must stay in bed for several days after major abdominal surgery. Her team wants a preventive measure aimed at the venous stasis component of the Virchow triad. Which measure is most appropriate?","choices":["Intermittent pneumatic compression","Inferior vena cava filter","Thrombolytic infusion","Warm compresses to the calves"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Intermittent pneumatic compression, sequential devices that squeeze the legs in turn, imitates the calf muscle pump and keeps blood moving, countering stasis. It is used especially when a patient is immobilized after surgery or hospitalized for long. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 71"}]};
